The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
ndmsg->nduseropt_family = AF_INET6;
ndmsg->nduseropt_ifindex = ra->dev->ifindex;
ndmsg->nduseropt_opts_len = opt->nd_opt_len << 3;
<text>
Reason:
These assignments fill the non-padding fields of the nduseroptmsg structure, but the three padding fields (nduseropt_pad1, nduseropt_pad2, nduseropt_pad3) are never explicitly zeroed. Since the structure resides in uninitialized kernel stack memory, the leftover data in those padding bytes is copied into the netlink message, causing an information leak to userspace.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
ndmsg->nduseropt_pad1 = 0;
ndmsg->nduseropt_pad2 = 0;
ndmsg->nduseropt_pad3 = 0;
<text>
Reason:
By explicitly setting the three padding fields to zero, all unused bytes in the structure are cleared. This prevents any uninitialized kernel stack data from being included in the netlink message, thereby eliminating the information leak.

